PHP File Uploading Screenshots

This folder contains screenshots demonstrating PHP file uploading, file renaming, file validation, and file handling in the course Web Application Development - PHP & MySQL.
The example uses an HTML form and PHP to select a file, display its details, rename it, check for duplicates, limit its size and extension, and move it to a destination folder.
1. PHP File Upload Form
Screenshot Name
Part 1.png
Description
This screenshot shows the HTML form used to select a file and enter a new file name before uploading.
Main concepts covered:
- action="Home.php" submits the form to the PHP file named Home.php.
- method="post" sends form data using the HTTP POST method.
- enctype="multipart/form-data" is required to send uploaded files.
- <input type="file" name="stdimages"> lets the user choose a file.
- <input type="text" name="file_Name"> lets the user choose a new name.
- The submit button starts the upload request.
Example Code
<form action="Home.php" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
    <h1>Select Your Image</h1>
    <input type="file" name="stdimages"> <hr>
    <label>Enter Rename File</label>
    <input type="text" name="file_Name"> <hr>
    <input type="submit" name="submit" value="Upload">
</form>
Important: The name attributes are used as keys when PHP reads $_FILES and $_POST.
Screenshot

2. Reading File Information and Renaming the Uploaded File
Screenshot Name
Part 2.png
Description
This screenshot demonstrates how PHP accesses uploaded file information using the $_FILES superglobal array, extracts a filename extension, and prepares the destination path with a new filename.
Main concepts covered:
- !empty($_FILES['stdimages']['name']) checks whether a file name was submitted.
- $_FILES['stdimages']['name'] contains the original file name.
- $_FILES['stdimages']['type'] contains the browser-provided file type.
- $_FILES['stdimages']['size'] contains the file size in bytes.
- $_FILES['stdimages']['tmp_name'] contains the temporary uploaded file location.
- explode(".", ...) splits a filename at each dot.
- end(...) returns the last element, which is used here as the extension.
- $_POST['file_Name'] reads the new name entered in the form.
Reading Uploaded File Details
if (!empty($_FILES['stdimages']['name'])) {
    echo "File name is :" . $_FILES['stdimages']['name'] . "<hr>";
    echo "File name type :" . $_FILES['stdimages']['type'] . "<hr>";
    echo "File name size :" . $_FILES['stdimages']['size'] . "<hr>";
    echo "File name Temp Name :" . $_FILES['stdimages']['tmp_name'] . "<hr>";
}
Extracting the Extension and Building a New Filename
$ext = explode(".", $_FILES['stdimages']['name']);
$ext = end($ext);

$temp = $_FILES['stdimages']['tmp_name'];

$Destination = "A:\JUST Docs\Semester 7 Docs\PHP & MySQL\PHP_test/"
    . $_POST['file_Name'] . "." . $ext;
For example, uploading Ahmed.jpg and entering Profile produces the intended new filename Profile.jpg.
Note: end() returns the last part of the split filename (jpg), not the first part (Ahmed). The destination shown above is the local Windows path used in the class example; it must be replaced with a writable folder on your own server.
Screenshot

3. Checking for Existing Files, File Size, and Allowed Extensions
Screenshot Name
Part 3.png
Description
This screenshot explains how PHP checks whether a file already exists, rejects large uploads, and allows only selected file extensions.
Main concepts covered:
- file_exists($Destination) checks whether the destination file already exists.
- $_FILES['stdimages']['size'] < 100 * 1024 checks whether the upload is smaller than 100 KiB (102,400 bytes).
- array("jpg", "pdf") lists the allowed filename extensions.
- strtolower(...) converts the extension to lowercase.
- in_array($ext, $exten) checks whether the extension is allowed.
Example Code
if (file_exists($Destination)) {
    echo "Already Exists";
} else {
    if ($_FILES['stdimages']['size'] < 100 * 1024) {
        $exten = array("jpg", "pdf");
        $exts = explode(".", $_FILES['stdimages']['name']);
        $ext = strtolower(end($exts));

        if (!in_array($ext, $exten)) {
            echo "Not Allowed File Type";
        } else {
            // Continue to move_uploaded_file(...)
        }
    } else {
        echo "Exceeds the limit";
    }
}
Possible Results
- Already Exists — the target filename is already taken.
- Not Allowed File Type — the extension is not jpg or pdf.
- Exceeds the limit — the file is 100 KiB or larger.
Important: The example accepts JPG and PDF, even though the form heading says “Select Your Image.” A PDF is a document, not an image.
Screenshot

4. Moving the Uploaded File and Showing Messages
Screenshot Name
Part 4.png
Description
This screenshot demonstrates the final upload step using move_uploaded_file() and the messages displayed when the upload succeeds or fails.
Main concepts covered:
- move_uploaded_file($temp, $Destination) moves the temporary upload to its final location.
- The function returns true when the move succeeds and false when it fails.
- echo displays feedback to the user.
- The final else displays a message when no filename was submitted.
Example Code
if (move_uploaded_file($temp, $Destination)) {
    echo "File Uploaded Successfully";
} else {
    echo "Not Moved File";
}
No File Selected
else {
    echo "Please select file";
}
Possible Results
- File Uploaded Successfully — PHP moved the uploaded file.
- Not Moved File — the move operation failed.
- Please select file — the file selection was empty.
Screenshot

5. Complete PHP File Uploading Workflow
Description
The complete example follows these steps:
1. Open the HTML upload form.
2. Choose a file and enter a new filename.
3. Submit the form to Home.php using POST.
4. Read the uploaded file details using $_FILES.
5. Extract the original file extension.
6. Combine the requested new name and extension to create the destination path.
7. Check whether the destination file already exists.
8. Reject files with a size of 100 KiB or more.
9. Reject extensions other than jpg or pdf.
10. Move the file using move_uploaded_file().
11. Display the result to the user.
PHP Functions and Superglobals Used
Function / Variable	Purpose
$_FILES	Reads uploaded file metadata and the temporary filename.
$_POST	Reads the new filename from the form.
empty()	Checks whether a value is empty.
explode()	Splits the filename into parts.
end()	Returns the last array element.
strtolower()	Converts the extension to lowercase.
file_exists()	Checks for an existing destination file.
in_array()	Tests whether an extension is in the allowed list.
move_uploaded_file()	Moves a valid PHP-uploaded temporary file.
echo	Prints messages in the browser.


6. How to Run the Example
Requirements
- PHP installed and running through a local web server, such as XAMPP or WAMP.
- The source file saved as Home.php.
- A valid, writable upload destination directory.
Example Project Location
XAMPP: C:\xampp\htdocs\PHP_test\Home.php
WAMP:  C:\wamp\www\PHP_test\Home.php
Then visit the appropriate local URL, for example:
http://localhost/PHP_test/Home.php
Ensure the destination folder exists and PHP has permission to write to it. The drive and directory from the screenshot may not exist on another machine.
7. Important Notes for Real Applications
This is a classroom example, not production-ready upload handling. Before using similar code on a public website:
- Check $_FILES['stdimages']['error'] and verify the upload succeeded before reading or moving it.
- Validate actual file content or MIME type server-side, rather than trusting the extension or browser-supplied type field.
- Sanitize or replace user-entered filenames to prevent path traversal and unsafe filenames; generate unique server-side names where possible.
- Restrict uploads to a dedicated directory, preferably outside the publicly accessible web root.
- Use a fixed, normalized destination directory and enforce size limits in PHP configuration as well as application code.
- Escape any user-controlled text before displaying it in HTML.
- If checking for duplicates, use a strategy that prevents filename collisions under concurrent requests.
Learning summary: This lesson introduces file uploads with HTML and PHP, the $_FILES and $_POST superglobals, file renaming, duplicate checks, size and extension checks, and move_uploaded_file().